Core responsibilities

Claim tethering

Review technical, scientific and commercial claims for sudden upward acceleration.

You will routinely encounter chains such as:

\text{Model passes benchmark}
\Rightarrow
\text{AGI}
\Rightarrow
\text{autonomous workforce}
\Rightarrow
\text{robot economy}
\Rightarrow
\text{post-scarcity civilisation}

Your task is to identify the precise arrow at which the balloon came loose.

Full-chain reconstruction

Ask technically inconvenient questions, including:

* Where did this quantity come from?
* Was it derived, measured or inserted because it looked appropriate?
* Why is \pi here?
* What is the complete dependency chain?
* Does the result survive contact with an actual physical system?
* Who owns the downstream value?
* What happens when the network disconnects?
* Why does the brake have an API key?
* Has anybody checked whether the cat was fed?

Physical-reality integration

Ensure that proposed autonomous systems account for:

* sensors;
* actuators;
* latency;
* control authority;
* local safety;
* component failure;
* maintenance;
* electricity;
* doors;
* furniture;
* animals;
* and the persistent tendency of wheelie bins to leave the operational area.

Statements such as “the cloud will handle it” must be followed until they terminate in either a valid architecture or an accident report.

Commercial gravity assessment

Evaluate whether capability claims have a plausible route to revenue, margin and economic capture.

You will distinguish carefully between:

\text{useful intelligence}

and:

\text{a profitable company capable of paying a \$600\text{ billion compute bill}}

You may be required to explain that discovering gold is not equivalent to owning the mine, and that inserting the word “marketplace” into a diagram does not automatically produce one.

Organisational air-traffic control

Monitor correlated balloon releases across research, product, finance, communications and executive leadership.

A single exciting result can generate a self-reinforcing formation in which one department cites another department’s optimism as independent evidence. You will interrupt these loops before twelve balloons become a weather system.

Constructive failure reporting

When a route fails, you will not merely announce:

“This does not work.”

You will provide:

* the exact failure point;
* the evidence establishing the failure;
* the assumption responsible;
* the consequences for downstream work;
* your assessment of whether the route is repairable;
* and the most credible next move.

The mathematics may be brutal. The work environment does not need to be.

